WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.073
<v bm_lewis>What if a board game were made of listening? Not a soundtrack behind a game, and not simply an audio adventure. Imagine a recording that is the game board, the clock, a witness, or even your opponent. Welcome to Play by Ear, an experiment in original tabletop games designed for ordinary podcast players.</v>

00:00:21.423 --> 00:00:41.720
<v bm_george>Two players might share a pair of earbuds, each hearing different secrets, while a third player watches pictures change on the screen. A QR code might send another player to a different recording. You might explore a place by jumping between chapters, or try to reconstruct a mystery from echoes, interruptions, and silence.</v>

00:00:43.070 --> 00:01:03.976
<v bm_lewis>For now, this feed is a laboratory, not a finished collection of games. The episodes test what podcast players actually permit. Some experiments involve stereo separation, rapid changes of chapter artwork, or branching between recordings. Others expose real limitations. The failures are part of the research.</v>

00:01:05.326 --> 00:01:30.768
<v bm_george>The longer vision includes cooperative games, listening puzzles, strange new uses of radio drama, and experiments inspired by sound art and Fluxus. If you are interested in designing, composing, programming, playtesting, or challenging an idea, find the Play by Ear GitHub repository linked in the episode description. For now, bring headphones, curiosity, and maybe a piece of paper. This is Play by Ear.</v>
